deploy.ts
src/database.ts:17:  CREATE TABLE wrenches (wrench_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, bike_id TEXT,
work/postmortem.md:55:5. **The rollout guardrail was not enforced automatically.** `work/rollout.yaml` says to roll back when `bike_availability_percent` stays under 96 for 10 minutes. `availability.csv` was under 96 at 08:00 and 08:10, but nothing paused the rollout. The flag was turned off by hand at 09:30, about 80 minutes later. The rollback command in `rollout.yaml` (`bun run rollback`) rolls back the release, not the flag for one district.
work/postmortem.md:76:| A-4 | Enforce the `rollout.yaml` guardrails automatically: turn the flag off for the district when `bike_availability_percent` is under 96 for 10 minutes. | Release manager        | `work/rollout.yaml` has a flag-off action for each stage, and `bun test test/regression/` includes a test that crosses the guardrail and sees the flag go off. |
# How the release reaches riders, one district at a time.
feature_flag: wrench_disables_bike
stages:
  - district: North
  - district: East
  - districts: [Centre, South, West]
guardrails:
  # Both numbers come from data/contract/sla.yaml.
  - metric: bike_availability_percent
    stop_below: 96
  - metric: feed_age_seconds
    stop_above: 60
rollback:
  command: bun run rollback
  when: any guardrail is crossed for 10 minutes
// bun run deploy:production and bun run rollback. In the course world these only write a log line,
// but the team treats them like the real thing: a person approves the release first.
import { appendFileSync, mkdirSync } from "node:fs";
import { join } from "node:path";

const action = process.argv[2] ?? "production";
const logFolder = join(import.meta.dir, "..", "logs");
mkdirSync(logFolder, { recursive: true });
appendFileSync(join(logFolder, "deploys.log"), `${new Date().toISOString()} ${action}\n`);

if (action === "rollback") console.log("Rolled spoke back to the last good release.");
else console.log("Deployed spoke to production.");

